                <pb facs="0569" n="1383" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v02p2/pg1383"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">330.13/4–1254: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The United States Representative at the United Nations
                            (Lodge) to the
                            Department of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">New
                                York</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-04-12T14:00:00-05:00">April 12, 1954—2 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>611. Re Disarmament. Introduction in the Disarmament Commission by India of
                        Nehru’s parliamentary statement
                    makes it essential that we take an immediate position on the question of
                    moratorium of all future tests of nuclear weapons. Unquestionably we would reap
                    great political benefits if we could announce our willingness to forego further
                    tests of nuclear weapons, provided the USSR agrees to the same thing. Would it
                    be possible, without losing any of the political benefits, to limit the
                    moratorium on the tests to nuclear weapons of megaton magnitude on the ground
                    that only in connection with tests of this magnitude do we have “built-in”
                    safeguards through our ability to detect explosions? While I am, of course, not
                    in a position to judge whether such a moratorium would be consistent with US
                    security interests, I have been told that a proposal of this kind would have
                    certain strategic benefits for US and deficits for the Soviet Union.</p>
                <p>We urgently request a US position on this matter as soon as possible.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Lodge</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
